# Who has to approve a change before it can reach main.
#
# The ruleset on main requires a review from a code owner of every path a pull
# request touches, and the most specific matching pattern wins. That is what
# lets review of ordinary code be delegated while two categories stay with the
# maintainer alone:
#
#   - The four version files. Bumping one IS the release trigger: merging that
#     change publishes to npm, to the VS Code Marketplace and to the auto-update
#     feed, using the maintainer's tokens. Reviewing code is one thing; shipping
#     it under someone else's name is another.
#
#   - .github/ and scripts/. A workflow runs with the repository's secrets, and
#     it sources scripts/products.sh and scripts/changelog-section.sh — so
#     editing either is arbitrary code execution with those secrets. This is the
#     line that matters most on a public repository.
#
# To let someone review and merge ordinary changes, add them to the first line.
# They need write access as well, or GitHub cannot request a review from them:
#
#   gh api -X PUT repos/VictorQuilgars/git-vertex/collaborators/<login> \
#     -f permission=push
#
# Contributors need none of this: anyone can fork, fix, run the products locally
# (see CONTRIBUTING.md) and open a pull request.

*                               @VictorQuilgars

# The release triggers — maintainer only.
/package.json                   @VictorQuilgars
/vscode-extension/package.json  @VictorQuilgars
/cli/package.json               @VictorQuilgars
/mcp/package.json               @VictorQuilgars

# Anything that runs in CI, with the repository's secrets — maintainer only.
/.github/                       @VictorQuilgars
/scripts/                       @VictorQuilgars
